Make read(2) on a negative file descriptor fail with EBADF.
mingw_read() emulates /dev/zero and /dev/urandom by comparing the
descriptor with zero_fd and rand_fd, which hold -1 while those devices
are not open. So read(-1) matched zero_fd and returned a buffer of NUL
bytes, with success, every time, instead of failing. A caller that
reads until EOF or error never stops: "patch -p-1", which reads its
input file from descriptor -1, hung while it collected NUL bytes into
one ever-growing line. With /dev/zero open but not /dev/urandom,
read(-1) returned random bytes instead.

Reject a negative descriptor with EBADF before the comparisons. This
also keeps it away from the C runtime, where UCRT treats it as an
invalid parameter rather than failing quietly.

---
 win32/mingw.c |    4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

--- a/win32/mingw.c
+++ b/win32/mingw.c
@@ -346,6 +346,10 @@
 #undef read
 ssize_t FAST_FUNC mingw_read(int fd, void *buf, size_t count)
 {
+	if (fd < 0) {
+		errno = EBADF;
+		return -1;
+	}
 	if (fd == zero_fd) {
 		memset(buf, 0, count);
 		return count;
